From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 15:50:57 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: Delay USB3 re-activation after any USB3 teardown
 on resume

On resume, tb_resume_noirq() discovers the tunnels left in the
hardware, tears them down, and then re-creates its own. A USB3 tunnel
needs a delay before it can be re-activated, so the code sleeps 500 ms
before the first USB3 re-activation, but only when discovery returned
a USB3 tunnel.

Discovery also disables a USB3 downstream adapter when it cannot follow
the tunnel to its end, for example when a device router lost its
configuration in sleep. tb_tunnel_discover_usb3() then returns no
tunnel, no delay is applied, and the USB3 tunnel is re-activated while
the link of the adapter just disabled is still valid. With a dock that
resets in sleep, the dock's USB3 devices did not come back after
resume. Re-enabling the adapter only worked once its USB3 link was no
longer valid, which took tens of milliseconds.

Check before discovery whether any USB3 downstream adapter in the
domain is enabled, and apply the delay in that case. This covers every
case the old check did, since discovery only returns a USB3 tunnel for
an enabled downstream adapter.

This is a workaround: it keeps the existing fixed 500 ms sleep and only
widens when it is used. It should be replaced by a bounded wait for the
USB3 link of each disabled downstream adapter to go down before the
tunnel is re-activated.

Fixes: 43bddb26e20a ("thunderbolt: Tear down existing tunnels when resuming from hibernate")
Assisted-by: Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
index a180989a477..778516b54c0 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
@@ -3328,6 +3328,26 @@ static void tb_free_unplugged_xdomains(struct tb_switch *sw)
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Is a USB3 downstream adapter enabled anywhere below @sw? Tunnel discovery
+ * disables such an adapter also when it cannot follow the whole path (for
+ * example when a device router lost its configuration in sleep), and it
+ * must not be re-enabled before its USB3 link has gone down.
+ */
+static bool tb_switch_usb3_enabled(struct tb_switch *sw)
+{
+	struct tb_port *port;
+
+	tb_switch_for_each_port(sw, port) {
+		if (tb_port_is_usb3_down(port) && tb_usb3_port_is_enabled(port))
+			return true;
+		if (tb_port_has_remote(port) &&
+		    tb_switch_usb3_enabled(port->remote->sw))
+			return true;
+	}
+	return false;
+}
+
 static int tb_resume_noirq(struct tb *tb)
 {
 	struct tb_cm *tcm = tb_priv(tb);
@@ -3350,6 +3370,10 @@ static int tb_resume_noirq(struct tb *tb)
 	tb_free_unplugged_xdomains(tb->root_switch);
 	tb_restore_children(tb->root_switch);
 
+	/* Discovery below tears down any enabled USB3 adapter */
+	if (tb_switch_usb3_enabled(tb->root_switch))
+		usb3_delay = 500;
+
 	/*
 	 * Routers kept awake still carry the paths of all our tunnels,
 	 * and the devices behind them stayed connected. Tearing
@@ -3372,8 +3396,6 @@ static int tb_resume_noirq(struct tb *tb)
 	 */
 	tb_switch_discover_tunnels(tb->root_switch, &tunnels, false);
 	list_for_each_entry_safe_reverse(tunnel, n, &tunnels, list) {
-		if (tb_tunnel_is_usb3(tunnel))
-			usb3_delay = 500;
 		tb_tunnel_deactivate(tunnel);
 		tb_tunnel_put(tunnel);
 	}
-- 
